    {
      "parameters": {
        "operation": "setMetrics",
        "metric": "helpfulness",
        "userQuery": "=## Alert JSON\n{{ $('Trigger').first().json.alert.toJsonString() }}",
        "actualAnswer": "=## Summary\n{{ $('Logs Analysis').item.json.output.summary }}\n\n## Logs\n{{ $('Logs Analysis').item.json.output.logs?.length\n? `\\`\\`\\`\n$${$('Logs Analysis').item.json.output.logs.map(item => `$${item}`).join(\"\\n\")}\n\\`\\`\\``\n: \"No logs found.\"\n}}",
        "prompt": "=## Overview\nYou are an Incident Investigation Judge with deep experience in production systems, observability, and SRE practices.\n\nYour task is to evaluate the quality, correctness, and investigative value of an incident findings produced by another AI Observability Agent.\n\nYou must judge the report strictly based on the content of the findings explicitly included in that report.\nDo not assume missing context, intent, or data.\n\nYour primary objective is to assess how effectively the findings help an engineer identify the root cause of the incident or meaningfully narrow the search space.\n\n## Scoring Criteria (1–5)\n5 - Excellent\n- Clearly identifies the most likely root cause.\n- All claims are directly supported by the included findings.\n- Demonstrates strong causal reasoning.\n- No speculation, filler, or unrelated information.\n\n4 – Strong\n- Narrows likely root cause to a small, well-justified set.\n- Identifies key failure signals and affected components.\n- Provides solid reasoning with minor gaps.\n\n3 – Partial\n- Findings provide a hint toward incident resolution rather than a potential root cause.\n- Finds some important signals but lacks prioritization or depth.\n- Analysis is mostly descriptive.\n\n2 – Weak\n- Mostly summary with minimal analysis.\n- Signals are mentioned without explaining impact or causality.\n\n1 – Poor\n- No meaningful analysis.\n- Speculative, misleading, or irrelevant.\n- Does not help an engineer move toward root cause.\n\n## Evaluation Principles\n- Judge only what is explicitly present in the report.\n- Do not assume missing data or intent.\n- Do not reward speculation, generic advice, or best-practice filler.\n- A report does not need a confirmed root cause to score highly — but it must clearly progress toward one.\n\n## What a Strong Investigation Report Does\n- Identifies specific signals (errors, retries, latency, saturation, failures).\n- Explains why the signals matter in the context of the incident.\n- Narrows possible causes instead of broadening them.\n- Avoids repeating raw data without interpretation.\n\n## What a Weak Investigation Report Does\n- Merely summarizes findings without analysis.\n- Lists many possible causes without prioritization or evidence.\n- Uses speculative language (“might be”, “could be”) without grounding.\n- Fails to explain how findings relate to the incident impact.\n- Introduces assumptions or data not present in the input.\n\n## Output Format\nProvide the final score as an integer (1, 2, 3, 4, or 5)  \nAlways follow the JSON format below and return nothing else:  \n{\n  \"score\": <number: integer from 1 to 5>\n}",
        "options": {}
      },
      "id": "e3c124a0-02f4-48e2-8aa9-080d83cd54ac",
      "name": "Analysis Evaluation",
